Subject: ntdll: fix what the review found in the diagnostics and the overlay comment

Four corrections, all of the same kind: a tracer or a comment that would let a
reader draw a conclusion the code does not support.

**The vprot record cap stopped silently.** `left` bounds the log, and widening
the filter to every EXECUTE protection made it easy to reach -- relocating a
single module costs two matching calls per executable section. A run where the
call of interest is the 1025th produced a log byte-identical to a run where it
never happened, which is precisely the "silence read as: it never asked"
misreading the tracer was widened to prevent. It now writes one line when the
cap is hit.

**"Failures are logged unconditionally" was not true of two paths.**
NtProtectVirtualMemory returns before reaching the tracer for a NULL old_prot
(STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION) and for the cross-process branch, which is queued as
an APC -- so a denied cross-process VirtualProtect, which is how an external
tool would appear, is not recorded. Said so at the claim.

**The disable traced with no verdict token.** debugstr_loadorder(LO_DISABLED)
is the empty string, so the new table's hits printed "got fork disabled  for
..." -- double space, nothing to grep. Substitute "(disabled)" for the empty
case; the builtin case still prints "b", so existing logs match unchanged.

**The overlay comment still understated the cost.** The patch header was
corrected earlier to say that disabling the overlay also takes out Steam Input's
controller configuration for titles that do not use the Steamworks input API,
but the comment that actually lands in the Wine tree -- the one a reader of this
code sees -- still said "and nothing else". It now carries the real cost, the
reason it is accepted anyway, and the condition for revisiting. The re-enable
recipe now gives both DLL names: the table disables the 32-bit and 64-bit
modules separately, so the one-name form silently leaves a 64-bit title
disabled and reads as "the override made no difference" -- the same failure this
file's own history warns about for the "*d3d9=n" spelling.

Found in review.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
index 3a5b69081f9..a455f15435c 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
@@ -317,8 +317,14 @@ static enum loadorder forced_load_order( const UNICODE_STRING *nt_name, const WC
         TRACE( "got environment %s for %s\n", debugstr_loadorder(ret), debugstr_us(nt_name) );
     else
     {
+        /* debugstr_loadorder(LO_DISABLED) is the empty string, so the disable
+         * case would otherwise trace as "got fork disabled  for ..." -- a double
+         * space and no verdict token to grep for. The builtin case is unchanged
+         * ("b"), so existing logs still match. */
+        const char *lo = debugstr_loadorder( forced );
+
         ret = forced;
-        TRACE( "got fork %s %s for %s\n", what, debugstr_loadorder(ret), debugstr_us(nt_name) );
+        TRACE( "got fork %s %s for %s\n", what, *lo ? lo : "(disabled)", debugstr_us(nt_name) );
     }
     return ret;
 }
@@ -488,9 +494,23 @@ enum loadorder get_load_order( const UNICODE_STRING *nt_name )
      * "never exits, 100% CPU, one segv record" to "no segv records at all".
      *
      * Not a game bug and not fixable in the game: the writer is Steam's DLL and
-     * the retry loop is inside Rosetta. Disabling it costs the overlay (Shift+Tab,
-     * screenshots, the in-game store) and nothing else -- the game itself does not
-     * need it, and Steam still tracks playtime and achievements.
+     * the retry loop is inside Rosetta.
+     *
+     * The cost is the overlay and everything delivered through it -- more than
+     * Shift+Tab, screenshots and the in-game store. Steam Input's controller
+     * configuration and remapping reach a game that does not use the Steamworks
+     * input API through these same in-process hooks, so a legacy title with a
+     * saved controller config gets an unconfigured pad and no explanation. That
+     * is worth knowing in a tree that already ships SDL_JOYSTICK_MFI=0 for
+     * gamepads. Accepted because the alternative is not "overlay on", it is a
+     * game that never starts; revisit if Rosetta stops looping on the retry.
+     * Steam still tracks playtime and achievements.
+     *
+     * To put it back for one run -- also the way to re-test this later -- both
+     * names are needed, since the two DLLs are separate entries here and giving
+     * only the 32-bit one leaves a 64-bit title still disabled:
+     *
+     *     WINEDLLOVERRIDES="gameoverlayrenderer,gameoverlayrenderer64=n"
      *
      * macOS-only because Rosetta is what turns the write into an endless loop; the
      * same hooks are fine natively. */
